<code>assert !this.isEmpty() => this.element().equals(this.getFirst());</code>

This assert statement checks that if the list is not empty, the result of `element()` is equal to the first element of the list. Since `element()` throws a `NoSuchElementException` if the list is empty, we only need to consider the case when the list is not empty.
--------------------
<code>assert this.isEmpty() || (this.element() == null && this.getFirst() == null) || this.element().equals(this.getFirst());</code>
--------------------
